## Context

A PR's `**Ticket:**` close claim lives in the PR body, but the branch content
can drift away from it: shared-worktree contention force-pushed an unrelated
collision-gate fix over `t0268-state-guard` (PR #522, 2026-07-13), leaving the
original `**Ticket:** 0268` claim pointing at work no longer in the diff. Had
it merged as-is, erg-pr-merge would have closed 0268 while landing an
unrelated change. The only defense was a human noticing and retitling the PR.

`erg-pr-merge` parses the Ticket lines from the body and closes them
unconditionally; nothing verifies the claimed ticket file relates to the diff.

## Relevant files
- `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` — parses `**Ticket:**` lines, runs `erg close`
- `scripts/check-cross-pr-ticket-collision.sh` — precedent for diff-based ticket checks in CI

## Actions
1. In erg-pr-merge, before closing: warn (or abort with an override flag) when
   the close-claimed ticket file is neither present in the repo `tickets/` at
   the branch tip nor touched by the PR diff. A claim on a ticket the diff
   never touches is legitimate (close-by-convention on merge), so the check is
   existence-at-tip, not touched-by-diff — decide the exact predicate during
   implementation; the #522 failure mode is "claimed ticket belongs to a
   different, replaced changeset".
2. Consider a cheaper tell: claim names ticket N but the diff touches only
   ticket M's file, or the PR title prefix and the claim disagree.

## Test
- Fixture PR body claiming a ticket absent from the branch tip → warn/abort.
- Normal claim (ticket open at tip) → unchanged behavior.

## Verification
- [ ] erg-pr-merge flags a close claim inconsistent with the branch content
- [ ] Happy path unchanged; override documented

## Invariants
- Existing merge flow for well-formed PRs must not gain friction.

## Exit criteria
- A repurposed/force-replaced branch can no longer silently close the
  originally-claimed ticket at merge.
